    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d644">

                <pb facs="0850" n="774" xml:id="pg774"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">611.5431/276</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Chief of the Division of Trade
                        Agreements</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Grady</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-12-16">December 16, 1935</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>I saw the President this morning by appointment and secured his approval of the
                    attached schedule on watches.<anchor n="31" xml:id="d644-f1"/> This schedule
                    calls for an average reduction of 33.8 percent as against the 29.8 percent
                    provided in our last offer to the Swiss. The President approved this reduction
                    because of the arrangements with the Swiss by which smuggling will be greatly
                    reduced, if not eliminated, and of the provision for the reduction in the duty
                    on parts, which will be helpful to the domestic manufacturers. He urged that, if
                    possible, we get, after the agreement is announced, some statement from watch
                    interests approving the arrangement regarding the smuggling provisions and
                    reductions of duties on watches in the Swiss agreement.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Henry F. Grady</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
